---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page, same shape and same rules as
 * stronglifts-5x5-tracker.astro and fitnotes-alternative.astro. Read the
 * StrongLifts file's header before editing this one; the reasoning is
 * identical.
 *
 * This page was deliberately PARKED until iOS shipped (spec section 4 of
 * badger-fit docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md, "at
 * the iOS launch moment"), because until 2026-09-09 the honest answer to
 * "FitNotes on iPhone" ended at "there isn't one, and nor is there a Badger".
 * It is built now because Badger is on the App Store.
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: FitNotes is someone else's app. Everything here
 * is phrased as compatibility (Badger reads the backup file their app writes),
 * never as affiliation or endorsement, and never as a criticism of their app.
 * The searcher arriving here LIKES FitNotes - that is why they typed its name -
 * so a page that opens by running it down loses them in one sentence. Do not
 * reword the title or h1 into anything that reads as "FitNotes for iPhone",
 * which would claim to BE their app on a platform they do not ship to.
 *
 * Division of labor with the sibling page, so neither grows into the other:
 * /guides/fitnotes-alternative/ owns the full account of what the import
 * carries. THIS page owns the phone-switch mechanics, which is the part that
 * page has no reason to cover: getting the file off an Android phone you may be
 * about to sell, and onto an iPhone. The "what comes across" section here is a
 * deliberate summary that links there rather than repeating the table.
 *
 * Claims checked against the app source on 2026-09-12, app version 1.1.11:
 *   - no iOS release of FitNotes exists (external fact, and the reason for the
 *     page; nothing in our code to check)
 *   - the parser and importer have NO platform branch at all: no Platform.is,
 *     no defaultTargetPlatform anywhere under lib/features/import/. The file is
 *     read with dart:io File and sqflite, both of which are first-class on iOS,
 *     so the iPhone import is the same code path as the Android one
 *     (fitnotes_parser.dart, fitnotes_importer.dart)
 *   - the picker is FileType.any, which on iOS is the system Files picker, so
 *     any location Files can see works (fitnotes_import_screen.dart:75)
 *   - in-app path: Settings, Your data, Import from FitNotes
 *     (settings_screen.dart:1084, under the "Your data" section header), and
 *     the same import is offered in first-launch onboarding
 *     (onboarding_screen.dart:339)
 *   - re-importing ADDS overlapping sets again (duplicates) and the preview
 *     warns about overlapping days (fitnotes_importer.dart, import_overlap.dart;
 *     corrected 2026-09-23)
 *   - iPhone only, iOS 15.0 or later: TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY = 1 and
 *     IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET = 15.0 in ios/Runner.xcodeproj, which matches
 *     what the App Store listing's own Compatibility section says
 * Note for a future editor: the app's own settings row calls the FitNotes file
 * "a FitNotes CSV export", which is wrong - it is a SQLite database. Do not
 * copy that wording onto the site to match; the site is right and the app row
 * is the thing to fix.
 */

const description =
  'FitNotes is an Android app and has no iPhone version. Here is what to do with the log you already have: Badger is on the App Store and reads the .fitnotes backup file directly.';

const modified = '2026-09-24';
---

<HelpLayout title="FitNotes on iPhone" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="FitNotes on iPhone"
    description={description}
    dateModified={modified}
    about={['FitNotes', 'iPhone', 'Workout tracking']}
    breadcrumb={[
      { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' },
      { name: 'Switching to Badger', url: '/guides/switching/' },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>FitNotes on iPhone</h1>

  <p>
    There is no iPhone version of FitNotes; it is an Android app. Your log does not have to stay on
    the old phone, though. Badger is on the App Store and reads the FitNotes backup file directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-log">Your log can come with you</h2>

  <p>
    FitNotes backs up to a <code>.fitnotes</code> file, and Badger imports it on the iPhone exactly
    as it does on Android. The move is three steps: back up while you still have the Android phone,
    get the file onto the iPhone, and import it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Back up before you let go of the Android phone.</strong> In FitNotes, go to
      <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup</strong> and write a <code>.fitnotes</code> file. Do it before
      you wipe or trade in the phone: Apple's switch-to-iPhone tools do not move a third-party Android
      app's data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Move the file to the iPhone.</strong> Email it to yourself, drop it in iCloud Drive,
      Google Drive, Dropbox or OneDrive, or copy it over with a computer. AirDrop will not work from
      Android. Anywhere the iPhone's Files app can reach is fine.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Install Badger</strong> from the App Store.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Import.</strong> Go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from
      FitNotes</strong>, or pick Import during first-launch onboarding, and browse to the file in the
      Files picker.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the preview, then import.</strong> Set the weight unit the backup holds: Badger
      assumes kilograms, which is what FitNotes normally stores. A backup from before 2018 carries its
      own unit on each set.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Import each backup once. The full steps, including what happens when a file overlaps days you
    already logged, are in <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">importing from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-comes">What comes across</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Your categories, with their colors, and your exercise library inside them.</li>
    <li>Every set, with its date, weight, reps, distance, time and any comment on it.</li>
    <li>Your body weight history, and your custom measurements with their units.</li>
    <li>Your records, worked out again from the history, so the graphs start out full.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Two things do not: warmups arrive as working sets, because FitNotes does not mark them, and you
    set up your routines again in Badger. <a href="/guides/fitnotes-alternative/">Moving to Badger from FitNotes</a> has the full
    list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-youre-getting">What you are switching to</h2>

  <p>
    Badger is a training log with no account, no subscription and no ads. On top of logging, you get:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Routines that pre-fill each session with your last weights, and
      <a href="/programs/">built-in programs</a>. Six of them propose each session's weight from their
      own rules, and you have the final say.
    </li>
    <li>Records per machine and per gym.</li>
    <li>A plate calculator and a warmup generator that work from the plates your gym owns.</li>
    <li>A rest timer that starts itself.</li>
    <li><a href="/help/progress/">Graphs</a> for every exercise, including estimated 1RM.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Every set exports to CSV, and the whole database backs up to a file you keep. Routines, exercises
    and equipment share as <code>.badger</code> files, a <a href="/badger-format/">documented
    format</a> you can read in any text editor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">What it needs</h2>

  <p>
    An iPhone running iOS 15 or later. Badger is an iPhone app only; there is no iPad version. It is
    free, with no paid tier; <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">the no-subscription
    guide</a> compares it with the trackers people usually weigh up.
  </p>

  <p>
    Badger is on Google Play too, and its own backup file moves your whole log between the two
    platforms if you switch again. <a href="/help/phone-migration/">Moving to a new phone</a> covers
    that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    FitNotes is the work of its own developer, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
    sponsored by, or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility only: Badger reads
    the backup file that FitNotes writes.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The section this page sits in, named above the h1 so a reader landing here
     from a search knows there are sibling pages for the other apps. Same as
     fitnotes-alternative.astro. */
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Matches the sibling pages: a disclaimer should read as one rather than
     competing with the guidance above it. Muted is AA-checked in both themes. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
